#### iPad / iPhone / Android (mobile)

1. Install **NetBird** from the App Store (iOS) / Play Store (Android).
2. Open the app. Accept the VPN configuration prompt.
3. Tap "Setup with Setup Key" (some app versions: "Self-hosted").
4. Enter:
   - **Management URL:** `{{ netbird_site_url_map.get(site, 'https://netbird.' ~ site ~ '.net') }}`
   - **Setup key:** `{{ _key.key | default('<KEY>') }}`
5. Tap Connect. Green toggle = on the mesh.

Note: iOS/Android have one active VPN slot. Disable any other VPN profile before enabling NetBird.

#### macOS / Linux (desktop, CLI)

> **On a Windows laptop? Skip this section — use "Windows (native)" below.**
> WSL is a Linux machine inside your Windows one, with its own network. Installing
> the client there enrols *WSL*, not Windows: the peer registers and looks healthy
> on the admin dashboard, while your Windows browser still cannot reach any mesh
> service. It also spends an enrolment off your key. Only install under WSL if you
> specifically want WSL on the mesh and you know your key has a spare enrolment.

```bash
curl -fsSL https://pkgs.netbird.io/install.sh | sudo bash
sudo netbird up \
  --management-url {{ netbird_site_url_map.get(site, 'https://netbird.' ~ site ~ '.net') }} \
  --setup-key {{ _key.key | default('<KEY>') }}{{ ' \\\n  --enable-rosenpass --rosenpass-permissive' if (rosenpass_enabled | bool) else '' }}
netbird status
```

If you're already on another NetBird mesh on the same computer, use a named profile:

```bash
netbird profile add {{ site }}
sudo netbird up --profile {{ site }} \
  --management-url {{ netbird_site_url_map.get(site, 'https://netbird.' ~ site ~ '.net') }} \
  --setup-key {{ _key.key | default('<KEY>') }}{{ ' \\\n  --enable-rosenpass --rosenpass-permissive' if (rosenpass_enabled | bool) else '' }}
```

Note: the macOS tray GUI is broken for self-hosted profiles. Use CLI for profile management; tray is read-only status display.

#### Windows (native)

This is the right section for a Windows laptop. The Linux/macOS install one-liner above does NOT work on native Windows (no `sudo`, no bash) — and running it inside WSL instead puts *WSL* on the mesh, not Windows. Two paths:

1. **MSI installer (recommended)** — download `netbird_installer_X.Y.Z_windows_amd64.msi` from <https://github.com/netbirdio/netbird/releases/latest> and run it. (NetBird's Windows MSI ships with `_installer` in the filename; the bare `netbird_X.Y.Z_*` tarballs on the same release page are headless archives, not Windows installers.) Adds NetBird as a Windows service + tray app.
2. **winget** — `winget install Netbird.Netbird` in an Administrator PowerShell.

After install, configure the self-hosted management URL + setup key via the CLI in an Administrator PowerShell or Command Prompt:

```powershell
netbird up `
  --management-url {{ netbird_site_url_map.get(site, 'https://netbird.' ~ site ~ '.net') }} `
  --setup-key {{ _key.key | default('<KEY>') }}{{ ' `\n  --enable-rosenpass --rosenpass-permissive' if (rosenpass_enabled | bool) else '' }}
netbird status
```

(Backticks are PowerShell line-continuation; on `cmd.exe` use `^` or a single line.) Green icon in the system tray = on the mesh. The tray app on Windows allows entering the management URL + setup-key via UI as well — either works.

